/**
 * @page genetic_search_chapter Genetic configuration search
 *
 * @ref GeneticConfigurationSelector represents one model configuration as a
 * tuple genome. Tuple position \f$j\f$ has the value type and domain of search-
 * space axis \f$j\f$. Applying a genome writes every tuple value to its bound
 * field, then copies the complete @ref ModelConfiguration.
 *
 * @image html genetic_cycle.svg "Generation batching, score-to-fitness mapping, and population evolution."
 *
 * @section genetic_initialization Initial population
 *
 * Construction samples exactly `GeneticAlgorithmConfiguration::population_size`
 * genomes. Discrete and categorical genes use a uniform stored index;
 * continuous genes use `std::uniform_real_distribution` over their bounds.
 * The initialization generator and @ref GeneticAlgorithmEngine generator are
 * both seeded from `GeneticAlgorithmConfiguration::random_seed` and then evolve
 * independently.
 *
 * @ref GeneticConfigurationOperatorFactory creates type-erased crossover and
 * mutation callables whose tuple positions match axis order. The returned
 * callables own copies of their probabilities and domain tuple. The selector
 * owns the search space, configuration settings, initial generator, engine, and
 * all in-progress generation buffers. It borrows the configuration fields bound
 * by its axes.
 *
 * @section genetic_protocol Generation ask--tell protocol
 *
 * 1. At the start of a generation, `GeneticAlgorithmEngine::askGeneration()`
 *    returns every genome in population order and marks engine fitness pending.
 * 2. The selector returns the next
 *    `min(maximum_batch_size, remaining_genomes)` configurations. It records that
 *    exact slice cardinality and rejects another ask until matching feedback arrives.
 * 3. `tell()` appends feedback in slice order. The engine advances after every
 *    genome in the generation has received feedback.
 * 4. After the complete generation, successful entries must contain finite
 *    lower-is-better selection scores. Let \f$S_{worst}\f$ be the largest such
 *    score. Selector fitness is
 *    \f[
 *    F_i=S_{worst}-S_i+\varepsilon
 *    \f]
 *    for success, where `fitness_epsilon` is finite and positive. Failed entries
 *    receive failed status and zero fitness.
 * 5. A generation with zero successful entries marks the selector exhausted.
 *    A successful generation reaches `tellGeneration()`, which evolves the
 *    population before the next selector ask.
 *
 * This protocol keeps fitness association stable even when model selection uses
 * batches smaller than the population. A model-selection budget can end after a
 * generation slice only when its remaining allowance truncates the request; the
 * returned slice still receives complete feedback.
 *
 * @section genetic_selection Parent selection and elitism
 *
 * @ref GeneticAlgorithmEngine normalizes successful negative input fitness to
 * zero and excludes failed individuals. It sorts successful indices by
 * descending fitness and copies
 * `min(elite_count, successful_count)` genomes unchanged. Copied elites enter
 * the next population with empty fitness.
 *
 * Each parent selection builds a tournament of `tournament_size` draws with
 * replacement from successful indices. If tournament fitness sums to a positive
 * value, `std::discrete_distribution` selects proportionally to those weights.
 * If every tournament weight is zero, selection is uniform among sampled
 * participants. Two independent selections supply each child's parents.
 *
 * @section genetic_operators Crossover and mutation
 *
 * @ref UniformCrossoverConfiguration selects each child gene independently from
 * the first parent with `first_parent_gene_probability`; otherwise it copies the
 * second-parent gene. @ref RandomResetMutationConfiguration then considers each
 * axis independently. With probability `per_axis_probability`, the current gene
 * is replaced by a fresh full-domain sample. Replacement may equal the previous
 * value.
 *
 * Children fill all population positions after elites. The engine replaces the
 * complete population, increments `generation()`, clears its pending flag, and
 * waits for the next full-generation ask.
 *
 * @section genetic_errors State and error conditions
 *
 * Population size must be at least two. Elite count is strictly below population
 * size, and tournament size lies in `[1,population_size]`. Operator probabilities
 * lie in `[0,1]`. `askGeneration()` while fitness is pending and
 * `tellGeneration()` without a pending generation throw `std::logic_error`.
 * Cardinality mismatch or non-finite successful fitness throws
 * `std::invalid_argument`. The standalone engine rejects a generation containing
 * no successful evaluation with `std::runtime_error`; the configuration selector
 * converts that condition to exhaustion before calling the engine.
 *
 * @see @ref model_selection_chapter "Model selection and validation"
 * @see @ref technical_chapters "Technical Chapters"
 */
